<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haakon IV of Norway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1204">1204</a>–<a href="/years/1263">1263</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Haakon Haakonsson, Haakon the Old</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Haakon IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> reigned from 1217 and brought the long Norwegian civil wars to an end, defeating the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skule-bardsson">Skule Bårdsson</a> in 1240. His reign is seen as the height of medieval Norway. He drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> under the Norwegian crown in 1262–1264 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> after his campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in 1263.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haakon-iv-of-norway.jpg" alt="Haakon IV of Norway" width="320" height="337" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Drawing by Hakon Thorsen (d. before 1924) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kong_Haakon_Haakonsson_PI_III_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Haakon was born in the summer of 1204, probably in March or April, at Folkenborg to Inga of Varteig. His father was widely held to be King Haakon Sverresson, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkebeiner">Birkebeiner</a> faction in the civil war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagler">Baglers</a>, though he had died before the birth. Born in Bagler territory, the child was in danger. In the winter of 1205/06 a group of Birkebeiners fled with him, and two skiers, Torstein Skevla and Skjervald Skrukka, carried him over the mountain from Lillehammer to Østerdalen. The Birkebeinerrennet ski race commemorates the journey.</p><p>After King Inge Bårdsson died in 1217 a succession dispute followed. Haakon had the backing of most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkebeiner">Birkebeiners</a>, and in June 1217 he was proclaimed king at Øyrating. Inge's half-brother Earl Skule Bårdsson settled on being his regent during his minority. In 1218 his mother proved his parentage by trial by ordeal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Haakon and Skule drifted apart, and a great meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> in 1223 confirmed Haakon as king. He defeated the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagler">Bagler</a> pretender, Sigurd Ribbung, in 1227, and ended the civil war era in 1240 by having Skule killed, a year after Skule had proclaimed himself king. The pope withheld recognition because of Haakon's illegitimate birth; Innocent IV granted it in 1246, and Cardinal William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabina">Sabina</a> crowned him in 1247. He designated children of his marriage to Margrete Skulesdatter as heirs, bringing Catholic legitimacy into the order of succession.</p><p>At court he had European literature translated into Old Norse, beginning with the romance Tristan and Iseult in 1226, which helped begin the Norse chivalric sagas. He built a stone palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>, and his New Law of about 1260 advanced public justice over feud and revenge. Late in his reign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a> came under the Norwegian crown. He rejected Scottish attempts to buy the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a>, and when Alexander III attacked them he led an expedition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Haakon fell ill and died on 16 December 1263 while wintering in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, after engagements with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, and was succeeded by his son Magnus VI. The Saga of Haakon Haakonsson, written soon after by the Icelandic writer and politician Sturla Þórðarson, is the chief source, and is considered the most detailed and reliable of the sagas of Norwegian kings, though written in support of the House of Sverre. Under him medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> is considered to have reached its golden age, and the realm its largest extent. Historians have differed: P. A. Munch saw an almost flawless ruler, while Hans E. Kinck saw an insignificant king.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Haakon IV” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The overseas realm was tested in 1263, when <b>Haakon IV</b> led a great fleet to defend the Hebrides; after the inconclusive Battle of Largs he died in Orkney.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>On the Ayrshire coast near Largs, Scots fought part of the storm-battered fleet of <b>Haakon IV of Norway</b>, who sought to reassert Norwegian sovereignty over Scotland's western seaboard.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-2">2 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 13th century, the Sturlung Age, a few great families fought for supremacy, and the Norwegian king <b>Haakon IV</b> intervened.</blockquote><a href="/state/icelandic-commonwealth">Icelandic Commonwealth</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Folkenborg, in what is now <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a>. Died in Bishop's Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.54,11.31&amp;year=1242" aria-label="Folkenborg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4788 165 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Haakon IV of Norway"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5238 435h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5238 435h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.54,11.31&amp;year=1242">What was here in 1242? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
